WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.860 --> 00:00:06.470
KRISTEN O'LEARY:
All right, everyone.

00:00:06.470 --> 00:00:07.510
Thank you for joining.

00:00:07.510 --> 00:00:10.570
And welcome to Migrating
Cassandra Workloads

00:00:10.570 --> 00:00:13.370
to Cloud Bigtable.

00:00:13.370 --> 00:00:14.440
Brief introductions.

00:00:14.440 --> 00:00:15.770
My name is Kristen O'Leary.

00:00:15.770 --> 00:00:17.780
I'm a developer
relations engineer.

00:00:17.780 --> 00:00:20.810
And I focus on Bigtable
client libraries.

00:00:20.810 --> 00:00:22.570
BILLY JACOBSON: And
I'm Billy Jacobson.

00:00:22.570 --> 00:00:24.980
I'm also a developer
relations engineer

00:00:24.980 --> 00:00:30.160
focusing on Cloud Bigtable,
sample code, and tutorials.

00:00:30.160 --> 00:00:33.040
So Kristen's going to kick
things off in this presentation

00:00:33.040 --> 00:00:35.320
by talking about why you
might want to migrate

00:00:35.320 --> 00:00:37.780
to Bigtable from Cassandra.

00:00:37.780 --> 00:00:40.780
And different application
structures you might have that

00:00:40.780 --> 00:00:43.300
are fairly common in Cassandra.

00:00:43.300 --> 00:00:47.230
Then with all those applications
and potential applications

00:00:47.230 --> 00:00:49.810
there, she's going to walk you
through how you would migrate

00:00:49.810 --> 00:00:52.930
each of those to Bigtable.

00:00:52.930 --> 00:00:55.060
Then once that's done,
I'm going to walk you

00:00:55.060 --> 00:00:57.070
through all the code
and sample resources

00:00:57.070 --> 00:01:01.090
we have available to make that
process as easy as possible.

00:01:01.090 --> 00:01:02.995
So Kristen, why don't
you kick things off?

00:01:02.995 --> 00:01:04.390
KRISTEN O'LEARY: Thanks, Billy.

00:01:04.390 --> 00:01:05.950
So we're going to
start with reasons

00:01:05.950 --> 00:01:09.778
why you may want to migrate
from Cassandra to Bigtable.

00:01:09.778 --> 00:01:12.070
So we're going to focus on
just two different examples.

00:01:12.070 --> 00:01:14.220
The first is maybe
you're currently

00:01:14.220 --> 00:01:17.520
struggling to maintain
availability, durability,

00:01:17.520 --> 00:01:21.150
and performance of your current
existing distributed database.

00:01:21.150 --> 00:01:23.610
So some more examples
include maybe you're

00:01:23.610 --> 00:01:25.560
struggling with
stability at scale.

00:01:25.560 --> 00:01:28.980
Or maybe there's just too much
operational load on your SREs.

00:01:28.980 --> 00:01:31.440
Or you're just generally
dealing with overhead

00:01:31.440 --> 00:01:34.470
of managing replication,
repair, and things

00:01:34.470 --> 00:01:36.870
like garbage collection.

00:01:36.870 --> 00:01:39.630
A second point could be you may
want to move up the tech stack

00:01:39.630 --> 00:01:42.360
and spend less time
and money on things

00:01:42.360 --> 00:01:45.570
like managing the different
complexities of a distributed

00:01:45.570 --> 00:01:46.530
database.

00:01:46.530 --> 00:01:48.960
So examples here
include maybe you

00:01:48.960 --> 00:01:50.940
want to reduce the
total cost of operation

00:01:50.940 --> 00:01:53.040
of your infrastructure
and do things

00:01:53.040 --> 00:01:54.870
that were different
than before and things

00:01:54.870 --> 00:01:58.560
that weren't possible before.

00:01:58.560 --> 00:02:02.110
So now we're going to walk
into some technical steps.

00:02:02.110 --> 00:02:04.020
So of course step
0 here is you need

00:02:04.020 --> 00:02:06.450
to plan for your migration.

00:02:06.450 --> 00:02:09.389
So before you dive into a
migration from Cassandra

00:02:09.389 --> 00:02:12.180
to Bigtable, it's really
important to deep dive

00:02:12.180 --> 00:02:15.270
into Cassandra use cases and
gain a better understanding

00:02:15.270 --> 00:02:16.980
of how Bigtable and
how they're the same

00:02:16.980 --> 00:02:18.750
and how they're different.

00:02:18.750 --> 00:02:22.560
You should do things like map
concepts, schemas, and queries

00:02:22.560 --> 00:02:25.590
from Cassandra to
Cloud Bigtable.

00:02:25.590 --> 00:02:27.300
A quick tip--

00:02:27.300 --> 00:02:29.850
Cassandra primary
key, you can look

00:02:29.850 --> 00:02:32.652
at as the same as equal
as a Bigtable row key.

00:02:32.652 --> 00:02:34.110
That's a great
starting assumption.

00:02:34.110 --> 00:02:36.035
Billy is going to
talk about that later.

00:02:36.035 --> 00:02:37.410
But that's a great
place to start

00:02:37.410 --> 00:02:39.993
when you're looking and thinking
about this type of migration.

00:02:42.390 --> 00:02:44.820
Next, we're going to
walk through an example

00:02:44.820 --> 00:02:47.250
of a migration flow.

00:02:47.250 --> 00:02:51.240
So step 1, you can look
into doing dual writes.

00:02:51.240 --> 00:02:52.980
So you're currently
obviously writing

00:02:52.980 --> 00:02:54.760
into your Cassandra database.

00:02:54.760 --> 00:02:56.370
Now you want to
try and write both

00:02:56.370 --> 00:02:58.290
into Cassandra and Bigtable.

00:02:58.290 --> 00:03:02.410
So any new data will
go into both databases.

00:03:02.410 --> 00:03:05.470
Step 2, now that you have
new data in Cloud Bigtable,

00:03:05.470 --> 00:03:08.650
you want to copy over the old
data from Cassandra into it.

00:03:08.650 --> 00:03:11.170
So you want to do
copying of existing data

00:03:11.170 --> 00:03:14.130
from Cassandra into
Cloud Bigtable.

00:03:14.130 --> 00:03:17.230
Step 3, option, you can
compare between the two.

00:03:17.230 --> 00:03:19.960
So make sure that your data is
consistent between Cassandra

00:03:19.960 --> 00:03:21.770
and Bigtable.

00:03:21.770 --> 00:03:24.170
Step 4, redirect you reads.

00:03:24.170 --> 00:03:26.090
So we're writing into Bigtable.

00:03:26.090 --> 00:03:28.190
And now that the data
should be equivalent to what

00:03:28.190 --> 00:03:29.880
we had in the
Cassandra database,

00:03:29.880 --> 00:03:31.880
we can also start
reading into Bigtable.

00:03:31.880 --> 00:03:36.900
So redirect your reads from
Cassandra to Cloud Bigtable.

00:03:36.900 --> 00:03:38.150
Step 5, stop your dual writes.

00:03:38.150 --> 00:03:41.060
So now we're basically using
Bigtable to read and to write.

00:03:41.060 --> 00:03:42.340
No need to dual write anymore.

00:03:42.340 --> 00:03:45.200
So you can turn off the writing
into your Cassandra database

00:03:45.200 --> 00:03:47.640
and only focus on Bigtable.

00:03:47.640 --> 00:03:49.690
And Step 6, now you're done.

00:03:49.690 --> 00:03:52.620
And you can
decommission Cassandra.

00:03:52.620 --> 00:03:54.570
So next up is migration paths.

00:03:54.570 --> 00:03:57.270
We're going to talk through
four different migration paths

00:03:57.270 --> 00:04:02.100
that you may encounter and
how to proceed with them.

00:04:02.100 --> 00:04:04.970
So here are some examples of
common Cassandra migration

00:04:04.970 --> 00:04:05.960
paths.

00:04:05.960 --> 00:04:08.420
And they each have different
migration strategies.

00:04:08.420 --> 00:04:11.220
Again, we'll look at them
more in detail on each side.

00:04:11.220 --> 00:04:15.720
We're going to start with
batch load, global serve.

00:04:15.720 --> 00:04:17.640
So you may have an
application where

00:04:17.640 --> 00:04:19.990
you load data into one region.

00:04:19.990 --> 00:04:23.080
And then you may want to
replicate the data out to other

00:04:23.080 --> 00:04:24.550
serving regions.

00:04:24.550 --> 00:04:27.780
So in this example, the first
thing we would want to do

00:04:27.780 --> 00:04:30.340
is rewrite our batch
jobs to support Bigtable.

00:04:30.340 --> 00:04:32.380
So this job that does
all of the writing,

00:04:32.380 --> 00:04:35.970
you now want to support
Bigtable as well as Cassandra.

00:04:35.970 --> 00:04:39.730
Secondly, you want to rewrite
your API to support Bigtable.

00:04:39.730 --> 00:04:42.457
So instead of just
writing to Cassandra, now

00:04:42.457 --> 00:04:43.540
we're writing to Bigtable.

00:04:43.540 --> 00:04:45.623
And now we want to start
changing API to do things

00:04:45.623 --> 00:04:48.010
like read as well.

00:04:48.010 --> 00:04:51.450
Step 3, you want to run
your batch jobs in parallel

00:04:51.450 --> 00:04:55.830
to load the data, both into
Cassandra and Bigtable.

00:04:55.830 --> 00:04:58.670
And then finally,
once you're done,

00:04:58.670 --> 00:05:00.450
and once you feel that
data is equivalent,

00:05:00.450 --> 00:05:03.240
you can release to switch
your API layer from Cassandra

00:05:03.240 --> 00:05:03.740
to Bigtable.

00:05:06.615 --> 00:05:08.300
So you'll see here
in the diagram,

00:05:08.300 --> 00:05:11.450
first we had just the
Cassandra instance.

00:05:11.450 --> 00:05:13.387
And now we're writing
to Bigtable as well

00:05:13.387 --> 00:05:14.345
to serve the end users.

00:05:17.390 --> 00:05:19.690
So next step is when
downtime is acceptable.

00:05:19.690 --> 00:05:22.660
This is another common
application instance

00:05:22.660 --> 00:05:25.600
where your application you may
be able to accept some downtime

00:05:25.600 --> 00:05:27.260
and have a maintenance window.

00:05:27.260 --> 00:05:30.670
So this is an example
of how you can do

00:05:30.670 --> 00:05:32.120
the migration during that time.

00:05:32.120 --> 00:05:35.080
So first up, you want to
stop writing to Cassandra

00:05:35.080 --> 00:05:37.210
during your migration window.

00:05:37.210 --> 00:05:40.540
And then you just want to do a
big batch copy from Cassandra

00:05:40.540 --> 00:05:42.220
to Bigtable.

00:05:42.220 --> 00:05:46.750
And so once you've done that,
the data should be equivalent.

00:05:46.750 --> 00:05:48.490
And you can optionally
compare to ensure

00:05:48.490 --> 00:05:50.590
that your data is correct.

00:05:50.590 --> 00:05:52.930
And then switch over your
serving layer entirely

00:05:52.930 --> 00:05:53.870
to Bigtable.

00:05:53.870 --> 00:05:56.997
So now your API will
talk to Bigtable instead.

00:05:56.997 --> 00:05:58.830
And then you want to
decommission Cassandra.

00:05:58.830 --> 00:06:00.460
So you can see here
in the diagram.

00:06:00.460 --> 00:06:02.260
We've now switched
over the API layer

00:06:02.260 --> 00:06:06.440
to talk to Bigtable as well.

00:06:06.440 --> 00:06:08.540
Next is online
with time to live.

00:06:08.540 --> 00:06:10.588
So this is another
common workload

00:06:10.588 --> 00:06:12.880
where let's say you have
something like a cache or user

00:06:12.880 --> 00:06:16.600
sessions where you have data
that eventually will expire.

00:06:16.600 --> 00:06:19.400
So we'll use that
to our benefit here.

00:06:19.400 --> 00:06:21.110
So that, first up,
of course, we want

00:06:21.110 --> 00:06:24.140
to rewrite our code again
so that we're writing data

00:06:24.140 --> 00:06:26.440
into both Cassandra
and Bigtable.

00:06:26.440 --> 00:06:28.600
So now we're getting
all of our new data

00:06:28.600 --> 00:06:30.263
into the table as well.

00:06:30.263 --> 00:06:32.680
And then you want to wait until
your time to live expires.

00:06:32.680 --> 00:06:36.460
So once your data that was
only in Cassandra has expired,

00:06:36.460 --> 00:06:39.490
the two databases
should be equivalent.

00:06:39.490 --> 00:06:41.650
Again, you can do things
like do asynchronous

00:06:41.650 --> 00:06:43.690
callbacks to verify
that the data is

00:06:43.690 --> 00:06:45.710
consistent between the two.

00:06:45.710 --> 00:06:48.340
And then finally you can
decommission Cassandra.

00:06:48.340 --> 00:06:50.140
So again, it's the
same diagram, we've

00:06:50.140 --> 00:06:52.360
written to both
Cassandra and Bigtable,

00:06:52.360 --> 00:06:54.940
and then eventually
do the time to live.

00:06:54.940 --> 00:06:58.440
Both sets will
become equivalent.

00:06:58.440 --> 00:07:01.940
And last but not least, we have
online with persistent data.

00:07:01.940 --> 00:07:04.100
This is a workload
that's user-facing,

00:07:04.100 --> 00:07:07.340
semi-transactional, can't
accept any type of downtime.

00:07:07.340 --> 00:07:10.230
So how do we work in that case?

00:07:10.230 --> 00:07:12.260
So you want to, again,
rewrite your API layer

00:07:12.260 --> 00:07:14.720
to read and write
to both systems.

00:07:14.720 --> 00:07:17.240
And then ensure there's managed
consistency in the API layer

00:07:17.240 --> 00:07:19.330
just in case something is down.

00:07:19.330 --> 00:07:23.240
You want to batch copy and back
fill your data into Bigtable

00:07:23.240 --> 00:07:26.090
because, again, you can't
accept any downtime.

00:07:26.090 --> 00:07:28.250
And then you want to do
asynchronous callbacks

00:07:28.250 --> 00:07:29.130
between the two--

00:07:29.130 --> 00:07:30.672
again, and then we
keep saying this--

00:07:30.672 --> 00:07:33.080
to make sure that your data
is consistent and equivalent.

00:07:33.080 --> 00:07:35.590
And then once you're
satisfied with the consistency

00:07:35.590 --> 00:07:39.920
of your data, you can
shut down Cassandra.

00:07:39.920 --> 00:07:42.380
So we've talked about a
lot of different situations

00:07:42.380 --> 00:07:43.070
and examples.

00:07:43.070 --> 00:07:46.070
Now Billy is going to take us
through some of the actual code

00:07:46.070 --> 00:07:49.120
that you will use if you want
to do a migration from Cassandra

00:07:49.120 --> 00:07:50.431
to Bigtable.

00:07:50.431 --> 00:07:53.193
BILLY JACOBSON: Thank
you so much, Kristen.

00:07:53.193 --> 00:07:55.110
We've got a lot of
starting points from there.

00:07:55.110 --> 00:07:58.690
So from those paths
you were going to take,

00:07:58.690 --> 00:08:02.030
there are two major
aspects to your migration--

00:08:02.030 --> 00:08:06.490
migrating your queries and
doing your batch data migration.

00:08:06.490 --> 00:08:08.900
So let's first look
at migrating queries.

00:08:08.900 --> 00:08:11.423
And if you're using Cassandra
or any kind of database,

00:08:11.423 --> 00:08:13.840
you have a lot of queries
you're going to want to migrate.

00:08:13.840 --> 00:08:16.455
So you're managing your
tables with queries,

00:08:16.455 --> 00:08:18.330
any kinds of garbage
collection with queries.

00:08:18.330 --> 00:08:19.372
You're writing your data.

00:08:19.372 --> 00:08:20.610
You're reading data.

00:08:20.610 --> 00:08:22.410
And you might also
have analysts that

00:08:22.410 --> 00:08:24.540
need to perform
table-wide analytics

00:08:24.540 --> 00:08:26.190
and are more
comfortable with SQL

00:08:26.190 --> 00:08:28.650
than some of the
programming languages

00:08:28.650 --> 00:08:32.020
that we have client
libraries for.

00:08:32.020 --> 00:08:34.929
So I'm going to walk you
through a bunch of examples.

00:08:34.929 --> 00:08:38.000
And they are all available
later in this codelab,

00:08:38.000 --> 00:08:39.867
Cloud Bigtable for
Cassandra users.

00:08:39.867 --> 00:08:41.409
You can learn the
basics of migrating

00:08:41.409 --> 00:08:42.700
a lot of these queries.

00:08:42.700 --> 00:08:45.440
And all the code that's in these
slides will be available there.

00:08:45.440 --> 00:08:47.950
So don't feel that you
need to pause the video

00:08:47.950 --> 00:08:50.140
to figure out what the code is.

00:08:50.140 --> 00:08:53.350
You can find the link later.

00:08:53.350 --> 00:08:55.128
So let's look at
an example database

00:08:55.128 --> 00:08:56.670
just to make things
a little bit more

00:08:56.670 --> 00:08:58.950
concrete through these examples.

00:08:58.950 --> 00:09:03.350
So we're storing data about
mobile devices in a time

00:09:03.350 --> 00:09:03.850
series.

00:09:03.850 --> 00:09:06.600
So we have information
about their cellular plan,

00:09:06.600 --> 00:09:10.620
how much data each phone has,
if it's connected to the Wi-Fi,

00:09:10.620 --> 00:09:13.940
connected to a cell tower, and
which operating system it's on.

00:09:13.940 --> 00:09:16.930
And you might have a
lot more data in here.

00:09:16.930 --> 00:09:21.960
We also are storing the device
type, a device ID, and the date

00:09:21.960 --> 00:09:24.100
that that data is stored on.

00:09:24.100 --> 00:09:25.950
And we could store
that in the row.

00:09:25.950 --> 00:09:29.610
But also we've upgraded
it into our row key

00:09:29.610 --> 00:09:32.280
because this is where we
are doing our queries from.

00:09:32.280 --> 00:09:36.570
This is very similar to
our Cassandra primary key.

00:09:36.570 --> 00:09:40.070
So all your queries
for Bigtable are going

00:09:40.070 --> 00:09:41.270
to be based on your row key.

00:09:41.270 --> 00:09:43.970
Kristin mentioned
earlier your primary key

00:09:43.970 --> 00:09:46.580
can be pretty much
translated into your row key

00:09:46.580 --> 00:09:49.320
by just concatenating the
important parts of it.

00:09:49.320 --> 00:09:53.930
So here we have device
type, device ID, and date.

00:09:53.930 --> 00:09:57.380
And Bigtable queries
are based on row gets,

00:09:57.380 --> 00:09:59.420
based on the row key
and row range scans,

00:09:59.420 --> 00:10:00.920
based on the row keys as well.

00:10:00.920 --> 00:10:03.620
So these features
are really going

00:10:03.620 --> 00:10:05.250
to determine which
queries are going

00:10:05.250 --> 00:10:09.270
to be the most performant.

00:10:09.270 --> 00:10:11.820
So to get started
with Bigtable, you're

00:10:11.820 --> 00:10:14.580
probably going to use our
command line utility, which is

00:10:14.580 --> 00:10:15.960
similar to the Cassandra shell.

00:10:15.960 --> 00:10:17.900
And this is called the CBT tool.

00:10:17.900 --> 00:10:22.140
Here I'm showing you how to
create a table with the CBT

00:10:22.140 --> 00:10:24.630
tool.

00:10:24.630 --> 00:10:26.460
And you'll notice
with Cassandra,

00:10:26.460 --> 00:10:29.100
you're used to defining
all the columns in advance,

00:10:29.100 --> 00:10:30.710
defining your primary key.

00:10:30.710 --> 00:10:32.250
And we don't do
that in Bigtable.

00:10:32.250 --> 00:10:36.155
We just defined the table
name and any column families.

00:10:36.155 --> 00:10:38.880
And you can define your
garbage collection rules

00:10:38.880 --> 00:10:41.890
at the column family level.

00:10:41.890 --> 00:10:44.820
So the CBT tool is great
for creating your tables,

00:10:44.820 --> 00:10:45.910
getting everything set up.

00:10:45.910 --> 00:10:47.970
But you can also use it
for reading and writing

00:10:47.970 --> 00:10:51.270
data, deleting data,
managing your app profiles,

00:10:51.270 --> 00:10:53.440
managing your
garbage collection.

00:10:53.440 --> 00:10:55.798
And if you have any scripts
for database automation

00:10:55.798 --> 00:10:57.840
that you have set up,
maybe using Cassandra shell

00:10:57.840 --> 00:11:00.060
or anything like
that, the CBT tool

00:11:00.060 --> 00:11:03.030
is a great way to
just swap those out

00:11:03.030 --> 00:11:05.130
if you're switching
over to Bigtable.

00:11:05.130 --> 00:11:07.260
But for your application,
you're probably

00:11:07.260 --> 00:11:10.020
going to want to use one
of our client libraries.

00:11:10.020 --> 00:11:14.460
We have our client libraries
available in eight programming

00:11:14.460 --> 00:11:15.850
languages.

00:11:15.850 --> 00:11:20.140
Go, Java, Python, Node.js,
just to name to few.

00:11:20.140 --> 00:11:22.590
And we have documented
all the different ways

00:11:22.590 --> 00:11:26.580
you're probably going to
want to use Bigtable there.

00:11:26.580 --> 00:11:28.950
So we're going to just
look at the Java client.

00:11:28.950 --> 00:11:31.760
But again, all those
other client libraries

00:11:31.760 --> 00:11:36.300
will be well-documented with
examples similar to these.

00:11:36.300 --> 00:11:39.680
So here if we're writing
data, in Cassandra

00:11:39.680 --> 00:11:41.000
you're used to doing an insert.

00:11:41.000 --> 00:11:43.770
You're going to specify the
columns and their values.

00:11:43.770 --> 00:11:47.190
Where, Bigtable, we do
writes as a mutation.

00:11:47.190 --> 00:11:51.200
So we'll take our row
key and create a mutation

00:11:51.200 --> 00:11:55.100
from that where we set a
cell's value so the cells are

00:11:55.100 --> 00:11:59.010
in intersection of the row key,
the column family, and column.

00:11:59.010 --> 00:12:00.920
And then we provide
a value for it.

00:12:00.920 --> 00:12:04.190
And you'll also note here,
we're providing a timestamp.

00:12:04.190 --> 00:12:08.570
Bigtable will let you
store multiple values

00:12:08.570 --> 00:12:10.470
at different versions in a cell.

00:12:10.470 --> 00:12:13.700
And you can use that as part
of your time series storage

00:12:13.700 --> 00:12:15.680
strategy.

00:12:15.680 --> 00:12:17.180
And with garbage
collection, you can

00:12:17.180 --> 00:12:19.010
determine how many
versions are kept

00:12:19.010 --> 00:12:21.468
or how old those versions are.

00:12:21.468 --> 00:12:23.010
So one of the parts
of your migration

00:12:23.010 --> 00:12:25.320
is going to be dual
writes potentially.

00:12:25.320 --> 00:12:27.000
So to do that,
you're going to pick

00:12:27.000 --> 00:12:28.960
your client library of choice.

00:12:28.960 --> 00:12:31.230
And then alongside any
of your insertions,

00:12:31.230 --> 00:12:33.000
you're going to
create a mutation

00:12:33.000 --> 00:12:37.760
to match that alongside of it.

00:12:37.760 --> 00:12:40.970
So now let's look at
two select statements.

00:12:40.970 --> 00:12:42.850
Here, we're just
selecting one row,

00:12:42.850 --> 00:12:45.130
because we're selecting
on all the parts

00:12:45.130 --> 00:12:46.270
of the primary keyword.

00:12:46.270 --> 00:12:50.835
So we've got the device type,
the device ID, and a date.

00:12:50.835 --> 00:12:52.210
And in Bigtable,
we're just going

00:12:52.210 --> 00:12:54.970
to translate that into a
row key by concatenating

00:12:54.970 --> 00:12:56.470
all that information.

00:12:56.470 --> 00:12:59.500
And here we're using
the pound symbol

00:12:59.500 --> 00:13:03.160
to separate each
piece of information.

00:13:03.160 --> 00:13:05.660
But you can really use
any symbol you want.

00:13:05.660 --> 00:13:07.910
So we take our row key and
then we just perform a get.

00:13:07.910 --> 00:13:11.130
And you can do whatever you
want with the data from there.

00:13:11.130 --> 00:13:14.790
So if you wanted to verify that
your data had been migrated

00:13:14.790 --> 00:13:17.970
correctly, you could query
Cassandra and Bigtable

00:13:17.970 --> 00:13:22.290
at the same time, wrap those in
an asynchronous callback block,

00:13:22.290 --> 00:13:24.600
and then log any
deviations that you've

00:13:24.600 --> 00:13:26.970
got until everything is
matching or you've sort

00:13:26.970 --> 00:13:30.110
out any of those errors.

00:13:30.110 --> 00:13:32.680
So you can also do
this for multiple rows.

00:13:32.680 --> 00:13:37.290
Here, we're scanning for all
the dates or all the dates

00:13:37.290 --> 00:13:44.000
after 2019, or just 2020 at this
point, for a specific phone.

00:13:44.000 --> 00:13:48.370
So with Bigtable, we can
do a prefix scan for that.

00:13:48.370 --> 00:13:50.565
We're going to, instead
of doing the full row,

00:13:50.565 --> 00:13:51.940
we just take part
of the row key.

00:13:51.940 --> 00:13:55.150
So we're taking the device
type phone and the device ID,

00:13:55.150 --> 00:13:58.072
which is 4C41, or
whatever it is.

00:13:58.072 --> 00:13:59.530
And then we're just
taking the year

00:13:59.530 --> 00:14:06.940
because all the data for
the device that's in 2020

00:14:06.940 --> 00:14:09.940
will have that as its
prefix for their scan.

00:14:09.940 --> 00:14:11.840
So we pass that
prefix to the scan.

00:14:11.840 --> 00:14:14.270
And then we can get
all the rows for that.

00:14:14.270 --> 00:14:16.130
And you can use this
in multiple ways.

00:14:16.130 --> 00:14:24.190
So let's say you want to get
all the data for 2020 and 2019,

00:14:24.190 --> 00:14:27.670
you can create a scan which
is two prefixes at once,

00:14:27.670 --> 00:14:30.670
and get all that
data back at once.

00:14:30.670 --> 00:14:33.290
And you perform queries
and create ways to do that.

00:14:33.290 --> 00:14:36.670
But if you're going to have
a little bit more nuance

00:14:36.670 --> 00:14:38.830
in your queries than what
the row key can provide,

00:14:38.830 --> 00:14:40.600
maybe just for one-off
queries or you're

00:14:40.600 --> 00:14:43.363
trying to learn new
things about your data,

00:14:43.363 --> 00:14:44.530
you're going to use filters.

00:14:44.530 --> 00:14:48.940
And this is very similar to
allow filtering in Cassandra.

00:14:48.940 --> 00:14:51.520
So we have timestamp
filtering, value filtering.

00:14:51.520 --> 00:14:54.190
You could do row key regexes
if part of the row key

00:14:54.190 --> 00:14:55.600
is going to help you out.

00:14:55.600 --> 00:14:58.000
But again, like Cassandra
with allow filtering,

00:14:58.000 --> 00:15:01.180
you don't want to let it
just do full table scans.

00:15:01.180 --> 00:15:05.740
So we recommend that you have
a range as part of that query

00:15:05.740 --> 00:15:09.560
or do a prefix and just
limit how much data

00:15:09.560 --> 00:15:12.760
you're going to be
scanning with that filter.

00:15:12.760 --> 00:15:15.520
And then a final option
for migrating your queries

00:15:15.520 --> 00:15:19.060
to Bigtable is to use
Bigtable as an external data

00:15:19.060 --> 00:15:21.370
source on a BigQuery.

00:15:21.370 --> 00:15:23.530
And this is just easy.

00:15:23.530 --> 00:15:24.530
It is really easy to do.

00:15:24.530 --> 00:15:27.910
You set up configuration file
to translate some of your data

00:15:27.910 --> 00:15:28.750
into BigQuery.

00:15:28.750 --> 00:15:30.970
And then you can do
table-wide analytics on it

00:15:30.970 --> 00:15:33.290
with a SQL-like language.

00:15:33.290 --> 00:15:38.020
So this is a lot more performant
for your table-wide analytics.

00:15:38.020 --> 00:15:40.000
You can save some of
the data after you

00:15:40.000 --> 00:15:42.835
query it to Bigtable to
hand that to an analyst

00:15:42.835 --> 00:15:44.710
if they just want to
draw on a lot of queries

00:15:44.710 --> 00:15:46.270
on the same set of data.

00:15:46.270 --> 00:15:48.070
Or we actually see
a lot of customers

00:15:48.070 --> 00:15:50.830
write data to
Bigtable and BigQuery.

00:15:50.830 --> 00:15:53.620
And they use the
data warehousing

00:15:53.620 --> 00:15:57.430
features of BigQuery
and the speed and QPS

00:15:57.430 --> 00:15:59.380
that Bigtable provides
as a database.

00:16:02.490 --> 00:16:05.065
So that's all about how
to migrate your queries.

00:16:05.065 --> 00:16:07.190
But most of the scenarios
that Kristin went through

00:16:07.190 --> 00:16:09.110
include a batch data migration.

00:16:09.110 --> 00:16:11.162
And we know this
can be intimidating.

00:16:11.162 --> 00:16:12.120
You have a lot of data.

00:16:12.120 --> 00:16:13.287
You don't want to lose that.

00:16:13.287 --> 00:16:15.600
You want to make sure
it all stays in sync.

00:16:15.600 --> 00:16:18.980
So we've made it super easy
with our bulk load tool.

00:16:18.980 --> 00:16:21.230
This is going to run
on Cloud Dataflow,

00:16:21.230 --> 00:16:25.190
our managed and hosted
data Apache Beam runner.

00:16:25.190 --> 00:16:27.440
It does an automatic
schema conversion for you.

00:16:27.440 --> 00:16:28.430
It will copy the data.

00:16:28.430 --> 00:16:30.568
And there's no coding needed.

00:16:30.568 --> 00:16:32.360
You'll just pass in
your configuration file

00:16:32.360 --> 00:16:35.423
with the tables, key spaces,
and server information.

00:16:35.423 --> 00:16:37.340
And then you can run it
with the command line.

00:16:41.210 --> 00:16:43.110
And what's great is
this is all open source.

00:16:43.110 --> 00:16:47.180
So if you want to take
our bulk load tool

00:16:47.180 --> 00:16:49.790
and make it work from some more
of your specific requirements,

00:16:49.790 --> 00:16:54.410
feel free to do
And on Apache Beam,

00:16:54.410 --> 00:16:56.630
we have the Cassandra
I/O Bigtable I/O,

00:16:56.630 --> 00:16:58.520
and even BigQuery
I/O connectors.

00:16:58.520 --> 00:17:00.440
So you can write
any pipelines that

00:17:00.440 --> 00:17:05.510
connect these three to
make whatever dataflow

00:17:05.510 --> 00:17:09.200
is going to work best for you.

00:17:09.200 --> 00:17:12.440
So now you understand why
you might want to migrate,

00:17:12.440 --> 00:17:15.800
how to determine the path to
get from Cassandra to Bigtable,

00:17:15.800 --> 00:17:17.930
you can do your dual writes,
stated verifications,

00:17:17.930 --> 00:17:20.579
and your batch data migration.

00:17:20.579 --> 00:17:22.770
So what are you
going to do next?

00:17:22.770 --> 00:17:24.290
If you're an
existing customer, we

00:17:24.290 --> 00:17:26.240
recommend that you connect
with one of your account teams

00:17:26.240 --> 00:17:26.930
to get started.

00:17:26.930 --> 00:17:30.470
They'll help connect you to a
lot of the resources we have.

00:17:30.470 --> 00:17:32.240
But if you're not,
you can check out

00:17:32.240 --> 00:17:34.410
some of these links we've got.

00:17:34.410 --> 00:17:37.082
Here's a link to the
codelab I mentioned earlier.

00:17:37.082 --> 00:17:39.290
So you can get started on
seeing how your queries are

00:17:39.290 --> 00:17:40.590
going to migrate.

00:17:40.590 --> 00:17:43.370
And you can even check out
a talk from Cloud Next 2019

00:17:43.370 --> 00:17:46.610
where Spotify discusses how
they migrated from Cassandra

00:17:46.610 --> 00:17:49.550
to Bigtable using a lot of
the techniques and tools

00:17:49.550 --> 00:17:51.060
from this talk.

00:17:51.060 --> 00:17:52.530
Thank you so much for attending.

00:17:52.530 --> 00:17:55.030
And happy migrations.

